  why did the census come in you know if  you you can kill dozens of people  hundreds of people thousands of people  and walk out after 20 years I think it  fundamentally break boils down to a  different way of viewing what what the  what the sentencing structure is the the  judges are from the court have a  different view of the purposes of the  court and of course many people have  different views about why this is but we  observe in criminal trials in Europe and  also for that matter in international  trials that the sentences tend to be  lower than what what many American  observers might imagine that maybe  because maybe we're imposing too tough a  sense on some of our federal cases maybe  they're not imposing strong enough  sentences and that's that's one of those  topics that people can debate for four  days what you mention in the book or you  talk about the numbers and the weakness  of numbers when we're talking about  things like crimes against humanity  genocide other types of you know  horrible acts why are the number so bad  well I mean you can look at the example  of the Congo a conflict where you have  from around 1998 to 2003-2004 you have  some 3 million individuals have been  killed who died as a result of the  conflict and those are enormous numbers  they're really impossible to to grasp to  be honest with you and and so it is an  incredible task to try to address those  sorts of crimes I mean it's  extraordinary complex the cases are  complex the defendants are hard to get  physically in terms of getting  jurisdiction over them it's a great  challenge of prosecute I think those  kinds of cases and so whenever you have  numbers like that in terms of millions  of people who have been killed in  genocides or in war crimes crimes  against humanity what collectively refer  to as atrocity crimes you're really not  only going to get the people with  command responsibility and arguably  that's what the purpose of these  tribunals is is to pick off the people  who really in charge and not the mid or  lower level criminals I mean one could  argue that that in an ideal world we  would we would be able to convict all of  them but the realities are not that way  are these the types of crimes that  should be adjudicated it you know by the  international community or does it  always come down to you know that you  know these these would be better  prosecuted in in different forums  altogether well the question is what has  both a practical dimension and also it's  a philosophical dimension on the  practical dimension and I'm speaking  just you know as a observer of these  things not in my official capacity  obviously I'm happy to see criminals  prosecuted by whomever wherever they can  be prosecuted in other words if you have  provided people due process you've got  you've investigated a case properly  you've given people a fair trial whether  that's done at The Hague or whether  that's done in Darfur matters less I  think then that it gets done now that's  the practical view maybe this is me  speaking as a prosecutor over  emphasizing the practical view the sort  of the more the political view of course  is that there is the International will  and and for a lot of people that is an  important symbolic significance that the  international community comes together  and decides that this person is  deserving of the being arrested and  prosecuted in an international forum and  being subjected to the condemnation of  the international community people can  differ as to whether that is or should  be a motivator as opposed to the  practical but from my perspective I if a  domestic jurisdiction can prosecute  cases well and fairly by all means I'm  all for it isn't that the case that I  say in the former Yugoslavia and Lord  hopes not the future Yugoslavia would it  be better to have the you know the  criminals tried there by people to the  degree that they can be impartial to the  degree that they can understand the the  state  on every possible level it just seems  like a better fit isn't it then removing  people to a to a distant jurisdiction  and you talked about the international  community but I mean who speaks for the  international community is its people  from bar for is the people from  Yugoslavia well in many ways in the  International Criminal Court is to  reverse federalism paradigm I mean many  people view the International Criminal  Court is being the the court that's on  top of all other courts in reality  perhaps a better way of looking at is as  a catch-all cord it catches those cases  that cannot be prosecuted and and  investigated properly in the domestic  jurisdiction at least that is what the  purpose of the International Criminal  Court is and so is it better to  prosecute a case locally or domestically  on the basis of the International  Criminal Court the Rome statutes own  articles and rules it is the the default  position is the case gets prosecuted in  the home jurisdiction of the domestic  jurisdiction if the jurisdiction is  either unable or unwilling to prosecute  the case then it comes to the  International Criminal Court when I say  the case we're talking about cases to  qualify as atrocity crimes and so the  International Criminal Court statutory  framework builds in the assumption that  is preferable to have it done  domestically what is the United States  position to the ICC and what should it  be well that's that's what I'm not I'm  going to have to punt on or rather I'm  going to have to I'm not going to be  able to answer just because in terms of  what the u.s. position is right now I  can tell you what what the formal  position is that that the United States  obviously is an observer has been active  in in helping draft the the articles and  rules by which the ICC is governed has  made public statements as to  prosecutions as to what the future role  should be that is something that will be  decided what we are not an active member  we're not an we'